    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d488">

                <pb facs="0605" n="507" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg507"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/2705: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-11T17:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 11,
                                1920—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 8:20 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>434. Your 227, March 6, 6 p.m., fully discussed with Foreign Office on 10th
                    instant, a memorandum being left embracing first portion thereof inclusive of
                    list of concessions and options specified by Japanese Government. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Aide memoire</hi> setting forth the Department’s
                    disposition as outlined in the succeeding paragraph of Department’s telegram was
                    left to confirm the conversation.</p>
                <p>Neither formula nor list have yet been received by Foreign Office from Japanese
                    Ambassador here or from Tokyo and communication with the latter has been
                    interrupted by break in cable. Comparison requested in Department’s 243, March
                    9, 5 p.m.<anchor n="68" xml:id="d488-f1"/> will be made upon its receipt.</p>
                <p>Although definite expression of Foreign Office opinion, which I am promised very
                    shortly, awaits study of this formula, I find its informal opinion to run quite
                    parallel to ours inasmuch as Japanese Ambassador was informed some time ago that
                    while railroad concessions <pb facs="0606" n="508" xml:id="pg508"/> in southern
                    Manchuria to the south and east of the South Manchurian Railway could reasonably
                    be considered to fall within the sphere of Japan’s special interest, those to
                    the west and southwest which approximate in their indefiniteness the former
                    Japanese interpretation of Eastern Inner Mongolia could hardly be considered in
                    the same light. I infer that the Department’s contention that such explicit
                    guarantee would impugn the good faith of the Associated Powers meets with
                    approval.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>What telegram is referred to in your circular of February 28, 1 p.m.<anchor
                        n="69" xml:id="d488-f2"/> by references to a telegram of October 5 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">15</hi>], 5 p.m. through Peking?<anchor n="70"
                        xml:id="d488-f3"/> We appear to have no record of it. Paris informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
